      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the given expression \\(\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!}\\), let's start by simplifying the factorial terms.\n\nFirst, recall the definition of the factorial function:\n\\[\n(N+1)! = (N+1) \\cdot N!\n\\]\nWe can rewrite the expression by substituting this definition:\n\\[\n\\frac{(N-1)!(N)}{(N+1)!} = \\frac{(N-1)! \\cdot N}{(N+1) \\cdot N!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we can cancel out the common factor of \\(N!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{(N-1)! \\cdot N}{(N+1) \\cdot N!} = \\frac{(N-1)! \\cdot N}{(N+1) \\cdot N \\cdot (N-1)!}\n\\]\n\nThe \\((N-1)!\\) terms cancel out:\n\\[\n\\frac{(N-1)! \\cdot N}{(N+1) \\cdot N \\cdot (N-1)!} = \\frac{N}{(N+1) \\cdot N}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can cancel out another \\(N\\) from the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{N}{(N+1) \\cdot N} = \\frac{1}{N+1}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final simplified form of the given expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{N+1}}\n\\]",
